  { name: 'Chihuahuan Raven',  url: '/species/chihuahuan-raven/' },
  { name: 'Nesting',           url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Chihuahuan Raven nest?', a: 'The Chihuahuan Raven nests in a wide variety of elevated structures in its desert and semi-arid grassland habitat. Natural nest sites include mesquite, yucca, and other shrubs and trees; cliff ledges and rocky outcrops where available. The species readily uses human-made structures including power pylons, telephone poles, windmills, and other utility structures — which are particularly important in the flat, treeless parts of its range where natural elevated sites are scarce.' },
  { q: 'When does the Chihuahuan Raven breed?', a: 'Breeding in the Chihuahuan Desert region typically begins in March, with eggs laid in April and young fledging by June. This timing places breeding in the late-dry/early-wet shoulder season. The Chihuahuan Raven is a colonial or semi-colonial breeder in some areas — several pairs nesting in loose association where food is abundant.' },
  { q: 'How many eggs does the Chihuahuan Raven lay?', a: 'Clutch size is typically 4–7 eggs — slightly larger than most other raven species. This relatively larger clutch size may reflect the semi-colonial breeding habit and the productive insect resources available during the breeding season in desert grasslands. Eggs are pale blue-green, blotched with brown. Incubation is approximately 20–21 days.' },
];
