    <h1>Chihuahuan Raven Nesting: Breeding and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven (<em>Corvus cryptoleucus</em>) nests in shrubs, trees, and human-made structures (power pylons, windmills) across the Chihuahuan Desert and surrounding semi-arid grasslands. Clutch size (4–7 eggs) is relatively large for a raven. The species can breed semi-colonially in areas with concentrated food. Breeding season runs approximately March–June.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven uses a wide variety of nest sites in its flat-to-rolling desert grassland habitat where large trees are uncommon:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Natural sites:</strong> mesquite (<em>Prosopis</em> spp.), yucca, cholla cactus, cottonwood, and other shrubs and trees — the highest available structure in flat terrain is preferred.</li>
      <li><strong>Cliffs and rock outcrops:</strong> used where available, particularly in the more rugged parts of the range.</li>
      <li><strong>Human-made structures:</strong> power transmission pylons and poles, telephone poles, windmill towers, and other utility structures are extensively used — particularly important in treeless grassland areas. The abundance of utility structures across the American Southwest has likely benefited the species by providing nest sites in areas that formerly lacked them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large stick platform, lined with softer materials including grass, wool, bark, and man-made fibres. Nest size varies with site — nests on pylons can be very large, built up over multiple years of re-use. Both birds contribute to nest construction.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Eggs and Incubation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Clutch size is typically 4–7 eggs, making the Chihuahuan Raven one of the more productive raven species in clutch size. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish-white with brown and grey blotching. Incubation: approximately 20–21 days, by the female with the male bringing food. Young fledge at approximately 35–40 days.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Colonial Nesting</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven can nest semi-colonially — unlike most raven species, which are exclusively solitary territorial nesters. In areas with concentrated food (e.g., near feedlots, refuse areas, or abundant insect resources), several pairs may nest in close proximity, forming loose colonies. This social nesting habit is unusual among corvids of its size and may allow more effective exploitation of patchy, abundant food resources.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

